NNAAMMEE
     nnddpp — control/diagnose IPv6 Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nnddpp [--aaccnntt] [--AA _w_a_i_t] [--dd _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e] [--ff _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e] [--ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e]
         [--ss _n_o_d_e_n_a_m_e _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r [tteemmpp] [pprrooxxyy]] [--VV _r_d_o_m_a_i_n] [_h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nnddpp command manipulates the address mapping table used by the Neigh‐
     bor Discovery Protocol (NDP).

     --AA _w_a_i_t
             Repeat --aa (dump NDP entries) every _w_a_i_t seconds.

     --aa      Dump the currently existing NDP entries.  The following informa‐
             tion will be printed:

                Neighbor
                        The IPv6 address of the neighbor.

                Linklayer Address
                        The linklayer address of the neighbor.  If the address
                        is not available, it will be displayed as
                        “(incomplete)”.

                Netif   The network interface associated with the neighbor
                        cache entry.

                Expire  The time until expiry of the entry.  If the entry is
                        marked “permanent”, it will never expire.

                S       The state of the neighbor cache entry, as a single
                        letter:

                           D       Delay
                           I       Incomplete
                           N       Nostate
                           P       Probe
                           R       Reachable
                           S       Stale
                           W       Waitdelete
                           ?       Unknown state (should never happen).

                Flags   Flags on the neighbor cache entry, in a single letter.
                        They are: local (‘l’), Router (‘R’) and proxy neighbor
                        advertisement (‘p’).  This field may be followed by a
                        decimal number, representing the number of NS probes
                        the node has sent during the current state.

     --cc      Erase all the NDP entries.

     --dd _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e
             Delete the specified NDP entry.

     --ff _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Parse entries from _f_i_l_e to be inserted in the neighbor cache.
             See the --ss option for a description of the file format.

     --ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e
             View ND information for the specified interface.

     --nn      Do not perform domain name resolution.  If a name cannot be re‐
             solved without DNS, an error will be reported.

     --ss _n_o_d_e_n_a_m_e _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r [tteemmpp] [pprrooxxyy]
             Register an NDP entry for the node called nodename with the Ether
             address ether_addr.  The Ethernet address is given as six hexa‐
             decimal bytes separated by colons.  The entry will be permanent
             unless the word tteemmpp is given in the command.  If the word pprrooxxyy
             is given, this system will act as an ND Proxy server, responding
             to requests for _n_o_d_e_n_a_m_e even though the node address is not its
             own.

     --tt      Print a timestamp on each entry, making it possible to merge out‐
             put with tcpdump(8).  Most useful when used with --AA.

     --VV _r_d_o_m_a_i_n
             Select the routing domain.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The nnddpp utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
